  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The ebony cup (Pseudoplectania nigrella) is a small, dark ascomycete fungus belonging to the Sarcosomataceae family, characterized by its distinctive cup-shaped fruiting bodies. Despite its plant-like common name, it is actually a fungus that plays an important ecological role as a decomposer in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Dark coloration",
      "description": "Nearly black to dark brown exterior and interior surfaces distinguish it from lighter-colored cup fungi",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small cup shape",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are typically 0.5-2 cm wide with a distinctive cup or disc-like form",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth hymenium",
      "description": "The interior fertile surface is smooth rather than ridged or folded, with visible spore-bearing structures",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ebony cup fungi are found on decaying wood, leaf litter, and rich soil in moist forests throughout New England, particularly in spring and early summer.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called the 'ebony cup,' this fungus is not a plant at all but rather a member of the Ascomycota, a division of fungi that releases spores through specialized sac-like structures called asci.",
